## Python and environment (all agents)
- Python {{PYTHON_VERSION}} is the project baseline; older versions are unsupported.
- On Apple Silicon, use native ARM64 Python and `osx-arm64` packages, not Intel
  (`x86_64` / `osx-64`) or Rosetta environments. Verify `platform.machine()`
  reports `arm64` before Python work. Create each environment with
  `CONDA_SUBDIR=osx-arm64 conda env create -f <file>` and persist
  `subdir: osx-arm64` in its conda configuration. (Delete this item on other
  platforms.)
- There are two conda environments. Do not use base/system Python.

  | Environment | File | Use for |
  |---|---|---|
  | `{{DEV_ENV}}` | `{{DEV_ENV_FILE}}` | Development: the minimum needed to import and change the code. No test, plotting, or optional framework packages. |
  | `{{TEST_ENV}}` | `{{TEST_ENV_FILE}}` | Tests, benchmarks, plots, and optional integrations: the development packages plus the testing and optional dependencies. |

- Keep `{{DEV_ENV}}` minimal to avoid dependency conflicts. Never install
  optional libraries into it; add them to `{{TEST_ENV_FILE}}` (and to the
  matching extra in `pyproject.toml`) instead.
- Before running Python work, verify `conda run -n <env> python --version`
  reports {{PYTHON_VERSION}} or newer. Activate with `conda activate <env>`, or
  prefix commands with `conda run -n <env>` in noninteractive shells.
- Update with `conda env update -n {{DEV_ENV}} -f {{DEV_ENV_FILE}}` or
  `conda env update -n {{TEST_ENV}} -f {{TEST_ENV_FILE}}`.
- Benchmark and paper runs record the interpreter, platform, and package
  versions with every result. Scripts that take the interpreter from `PY` use
  `PY={{TEST_PYTHON}}`.
- Include the environment requirement (which env, and why) in every delegated
  implementation or review prompt, including Agy, Codex, and Claude jobs.

## Cluster jobs
- Cluster job scripts live in `{{CLUSTER_JOBS_DIR}}` (its own repo and README).
  Long runs go to the {{CLUSTER_NAME}} cluster by preference. Use a new run tag
  for each release run.
- Never delete files on the cluster; {{OWNER_NAME}} does that by hand.
- Do not run heavy commands (`du`, `find`, Python) on a login node; submit them
  as jobs.

## Authorship
- Author/committer email must be `{{GIT_EMAIL}}` (set in this repo's local git
  config; don't override it, and never use {{FORBIDDEN_EMAIL}} or any other
  address).
- Do NOT add AI attribution of any kind: no `Co-Authored-By:` trailers, no
  "Generated with …" lines, in commit messages or PR descriptions.

## Commit messages
- Short imperative subject line (≤ ~72 chars), e.g.
  "Add caching for repeated solver calls".
- Optional body explaining what changed and why; no trailers.
- Commit only when asked.

## Branches and remotes
- `origin` = {{PUBLIC_REPO_URL}} (PUBLIC).
- `private` = {{PRIVATE_REPO_URL}}.
- Push routine work to `private` only.
- Never push to `origin/main` unless explicitly asked. When asked, it must be a
  fast-forward (no force push, no history rewrite), after checking:
    git log --format='%h %ae' origin/main..HEAD   # all noreply email
    git log --format='%B' origin/main..HEAD | grep -iE 'co-authored|generated'  # must be empty
- Run `git status -sb` before any push to confirm which branch you're on.

## Before committing
- Run the test suite: `{{TEST_COMMAND}}`.
- Don't commit .vscode/, AGENTS.md, secrets, or absolute local paths.
- Keep memory use bounded; benchmarks may use at most {{MAX_CORES}} cores.
