## Launching Agy and Codex jobs

Run these commands from the repository root. Read this file before dispatching;
include the task, owned files, verification steps, and "Do not commit or push"
in each implementation prompt unless the user has explicitly authorized those
actions. Launch delegated jobs only when requested. Give concurrent jobs
non-overlapping file ownership and preserve existing working-tree changes.

### Agy CLI

Exact executable path on this machine: `{{AGY_BIN}}`.
Use the quoted absolute path in place of `agy` below when PATH is unavailable.

Inspect installed flags and available model IDs before choosing a model:

```bash
agy --help
agy models
```

Interactive session:

```bash
agy --model gemini-3.8-flash-medium
```

Noninteractive job (substitute the actual scoped task). Headless `-p` mode
cannot prompt, so any job that runs shell commands (Python, tests, `ls`, `cat`)
needs `--dangerously-skip-permissions`:

```bash
agy --model gemini-3.8-flash-medium --effort medium \
  --dangerously-skip-permissions --output-format text --print-timeout 10m \
  -p 'Read AGENTS.md. Implement <task> in <owned files>. Run <checks>. Do not commit or push.' \
  > /tmp/{{PROJECT_NAME}}-agy-job.log 2>&1
```

- `--mode accept-edits` approves file edits only. In `-p` mode every shell
  command is auto-denied, and the job exits 0 with only this line in its log:
  `jetski: no output produced — a tool required the "command" permission that
  headless mode cannot prompt for, so it was auto-denied.` Exit code 0 does not
  mean the job ran; check the log and the expected output files.
- `--settings '{"permissions":{"allow":["command(...)"]}}'` did not allow
  commands in headless mode (tested November 20, 2026). Do not rely on per-command
  allow rules; use `--dangerously-skip-permissions` or an interactive `agy -i` session.
- With permissions skipped, file limits exist only in the prompt. State the
  owned paths and forbidden edits explicitly, and check `git status` in every
  affected repo afterwards.
- When launching from Claude Code, the outer `Bash` call to agy is gated
  separately by Claude Code's own permissions. Allow it with
  `"permissions": {"allow": ["Bash({{AGY_BIN}}:*)"]}` in
  `.claude/settings.json`, or approve the prompt.
- Model IDs above were available when this file was written; recheck with
  `agy models` rather than assuming availability or quota.
- `--mode auto` may be unavailable for an account and fall back to the default
  mode. Do not rely on it for unattended execution.
- Gemini Flash accepted `--effort medium` / `high`. Sonnet rejected the generic
  `--effort high` flag; omit `--effort` when using its built-in thinking mode.
- For an authorized unattended run, `--dangerously-skip-permissions` auto-approves
  tool requests; it does not provide filesystem isolation. Use it only within
  the authorized scope and an appropriately isolated execution environment.
- `--output-format text` writes nothing to the log until the job ends, so a
  running job can only be followed through the files it creates. For long jobs
  prefer `--output-format stream-json` (one JSON event per line, written as the
  job runs) and follow it with `tail -f` on the log.
- `--output-format json` is also available for a single structured result.
  Treat a timeout/interruption as incomplete work, inspect the log and working
  tree, and confirm the job has stopped before reassigning its files.
- Resume a specific job with `agy --conversation <id> -p '<follow-up>'`.
  `agy --continue` resumes the latest conversation; avoid that shortcut when
  multiple jobs are running. Sonnet and Google-model quota availability can
  differ; a Sonnet quota error does not establish that Google models are blocked.

### Codex CLI

Exact npm-installed executable path on this machine: `{{CODEX_BIN}}`
(global npm prefix: `{{NPM_PREFIX}}`).
Use the quoted absolute path in place of `codex` below when PATH is unavailable.

Default to `gpt-6.2-sol` for Codex jobs when it is available on the current
account. Check the [current Codex pricing](https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/pricing)
before choosing a model or speed mode based on cost.

Inspect the installed CLI before dispatching.

```bash
codex --help
codex exec --help
```

Interactive session with workspace writes and automatic approval review:

```bash
codex -C . --approve-for-me -m gpt-6.2-sol
```

Noninteractive implementation job with workspace writes:

```bash
codex exec -C . -s workspace-write \
  -m gpt-6.2-sol -c 'model_reasoning_effort="medium"' \
  --output-last-message /tmp/{{PROJECT_NAME}}-codex-result.md \
  'Read AGENTS.md. Implement <task> in <owned files>. Run <checks>. Do not commit or push.' \
  > /tmp/{{PROJECT_NAME}}-codex-job.log 2>&1
```

- Codex CLI 0.170.0 does not accept the legacy `--full-auto` flag.
  Use `-s workspace-write` (equivalently `--sandbox workspace-write`) for
  noninteractive implementation jobs.
- In codex-cli 0.170, `--approve-for-me` cannot be combined with `--sandbox`;
  it already implies the workspace-write sandbox. Pass `--sandbox` only without it.
- `--approve-for-me` routes approval requests through automatic review; it does
  not remove sandbox restrictions or guarantee that every action is approved.
- For a review job, select `--sandbox read-only` and explicitly request no edits.
- `codex exec --json` emits JSONL events; `--output-last-message` saves the final
  response separately. Keep each job's log/result paths distinct.
- Resume a specific job with `codex exec resume <session-id> '<follow-up>'`;
  inspect `codex exec resume --help` for installed options. Avoid `--last` when
  coordinating concurrent sessions.
- Use medium reasoning for routine adapters, documentation, and cleanup; use
  high reasoning for numerical algorithms, precision issues, and proofs. Choose
  the least costly suitable available model and bound review scope and runtime.
  Do not launch new model jobs merely to test these examples.

### Claude Code CLI

- Launcher: `~/.local/bin/claude` (on PATH), a symlink to the versioned binary
  in `~/.local/share/claude/versions/<version>`. The native installer
  auto-updates and repoints the symlink, so always call plain `claude`, never a
  versioned path.
- Auth and settings: `~/.claude/` (user), `.claude/settings.json` (project).
- If `claude` is missing from PATH (for example a non-login shell), use
  `"$HOME/.local/bin/claude"`.

```bash
claude --version
claude --help
```

Interactive session:

```bash
claude --model claude-opus-5-5
```

Noninteractive implementation job:

```bash
claude -p --model claude-opus-5-5 --effort high \
  --permission-mode acceptEdits --output-format text \
  --max-budget-usd 10 \
  'Read AGENTS.md. Implement <task> in <owned files>. Run <checks>. Do not commit or push.' \
  > /tmp/{{PROJECT_NAME}}-claude-job.log 2>&1
```

- Models: `claude-opus-5-5` (numerical algorithms, precision issues, proofs),
  `claude-sonnet-5-5` (routine adapters, docs, cleanup), `claude-haiku-5-5`
  (small mechanical edits).
- `--effort` takes `low|medium|high|xhigh|max`. Suggested levels:

  | Task | Model | Effort |
  |---|---|---|
  | Mechanical edits, renames, import fixes | `claude-haiku-5-5` | `low` |
  | Docs, docstrings, adapters, cleanup, benchmark plumbing | `claude-sonnet-5-5` | `medium` |
  | Numerical algorithms, precision, proofs in comments | `claude-opus-5-5` | `high` |
  | Hard proofs or debugging that `high` did not resolve | `claude-opus-5-5` | `xhigh` |
  | Code review of a work package | `claude-sonnet-5-5` or `claude-opus-5-5` | `medium` / `high` |

  Reserve `max` for an explicit request; it is the slowest and costliest.
- `--permission-mode acceptEdits` auto-approves file edits but still gates
  shell commands. To let the job run the tests unattended, allow exactly that
  command, e.g. `--allowedTools 'Bash({{TEST_COMMAND}}*)' 'Bash(git status*)' 'Bash(git diff*)'`,
  rather than `--dangerously-skip-permissions`.
- Review job (read-only, no edits):

  ```bash
  claude -p --model claude-sonnet-5-5 --effort medium \
    --permission-mode plan --output-format text \
    'Read AGENTS.md. Review <files or diff> against <design doc>. Report findings only. Do not edit, commit, or push.' \
    > /tmp/{{PROJECT_NAME}}-claude-review.log 2>&1
  ```

- Structured output and resume:

  ```bash
  claude -p --model claude-opus-5-5 --effort high --permission-mode acceptEdits \
    --output-format json '<task>' > /tmp/{{PROJECT_NAME}}-claude-wp1.json
  SID=$(python -c 'import json;print(json.load(open("/tmp/{{PROJECT_NAME}}-claude-wp1.json"))["session_id"])')
  claude -p --resume "$SID" '<follow-up>' >> /tmp/{{PROJECT_NAME}}-claude-wp1.log 2>&1
  ```

- `-p` jobs run in the foreground; background them with `&` or `nohup` and
  check the log. `--max-budget-usd` caps spend; a job that hits the cap stops
  incomplete, so inspect the working tree before reassigning its files.
- `--output-format json` returns the final result with a `session_id`;
  `stream-json` emits events. Keep each job's log path distinct.
- Resume a specific job with `claude -p --resume <session-id> '<follow-up>'`.
  Avoid `--continue` when several jobs are running.
- Claude follows this file's commit rules too: no `Co-Authored-By` or
  "Generated with" lines, and the noreply author email.

These launch instructions do not authorize commits, pushes, changes outside the
assigned scope, or edits to vendored code. Keep this local `AGENTS.md`
uncommitted as required above.
